WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.189
William D. Eggers is coauthor with

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.139
Donald F. Kettl of the new book

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.209
"Bridge Builders: How Government

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.979
Can Transcend Boundaries to Solve

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.719
Big Problems".

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.469
Now, executive director of Deloitte

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:13.199
Center for Government Insights.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.149
30 years ago, Eggers ran

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.129
the Privatization Center for Reason

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.079
Foundation, the nonprofit that

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.119
publishes Reason magazine.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.409
He's worked with dozens of

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.239
governments at all levels, both

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.319
in the United States and

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.549
internationally.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:28.139
And he's written a shelf's worth of

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.969
books on the proper scope and

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.499
functioning of government.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.059
I talked with him about Bridge

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.979
Builders and what he's learned over

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:36.989
the past three decades about making

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.789
government more effective and less

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:40.889
intrusive when confronting major

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:42.749
challenges such as pandemics

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.639
and why it's long past time to

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.499
move beyond what he and his coauthor

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.329
call "the vending machine

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.330
model of government".

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.869
Well. Eggers, thanks for

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:57.889
talking to Reason.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.719
It's great to see you again,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:00.679
Nick.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.019
We've known each other 30 years

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:03.828
now.

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:04.649
Yes, that is.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.189
It's kind of sobering, right?

00:01:07.190 --> 00:01:09.289
Your new book is "Bridge

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:10.579
Builders: How Government Can

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:12.529
Transcend Boundaries to Solve Big

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:14.419
Problems". You coauthored it

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.889
with Donald F Kettl.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.719
What's the elevator pitch of "Bridge

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:18.720
Builders"?

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:21.589
Basically, our political

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.779
and policy debates are mostly

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:25.639
based on an outdated mental

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.589
model of what we term

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.659
"the vending machine mental model

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.459
of government", which is you have a

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:32.659
big problem.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.519
You pass a law.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.889
You fund an organization.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.869
You put money into the vending

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:40.459
machine and you're expected to

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.139
produce results.

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.879
That's what we read about in the

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:44.779
newspapers.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:46.459
We see it on TV, and that doesn't

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:47.869
reflect either how government

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:49.849
operates or how you can actually

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.949
solve any big problems today,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:52.969
like take a problem like

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:54.409
homelessness.

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:56.239
Is it an economic

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.619
problem? Is it a mental health

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.629
problem? Is it a housing problem,

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.089
a crime problem,

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.039
or and whose responsibility is

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.049
it? Is it local government, state

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:07.369
government, federal government,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:09.619
non-profits, foundations,

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.029
the private sector care?

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.259
And the answer for all those

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:14.359
questions is yes.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:16.429
And so what we need is a new

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.499
model to look at public management

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:19.939
and government that reflects the

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.769
reality of a big blurring

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.299
of the lines, really, between the

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.189
public and private sector.

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.959
And the key to effective government

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.449
today is really the ability

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:31.759
to effectively

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:33.679
manage these public private

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:34.699
networks.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.069
Before we get into some specifics

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.019
about stuff, let's reflect a little

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.089
bit on your history, both at Reason

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:42.559
Foundation.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:43.729
When I interviewed

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:46.759
for a job and I joined in

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:48.469
October of 93, you were already

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:49.789
working there. You know, you're like

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:50.809
Bartleby, the Scrivener.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:52.309
You were there in the office when

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:53.629
the guy shows up in the office,

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.569
but you were running, or

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.549
you eventually ran a center

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.349
for privatization.

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:02.329
And talk a

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.669
bit about, you know,

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:06.559
when in the early nineties, when

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.569
your career started, what

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:10.609
were the challenges and what

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:11.749
has become because you're talking

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.699
about the vending machine model is

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:15.199
outmoded. That's not how governments

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.489
operate. What were they?

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.019
You know, how did that happen?

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:20.659
You know, I started off right out of

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.969
college working at

00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:24.499
Reason of what was then called the

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.299
local government center.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.999
And it was really doing the nitty

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.099
gritty of kind of

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.989
the local government management.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:35.359
And how do you deliver garbage

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.399
collection or wastewater treatment

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:39.109
in areas and, of course...

00:03:39.110 --> 00:03:40.129
The school buses, things like that.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.839
And at the time, people wanted to go

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.879
to think tanks and do sexy policy

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.439
things. And I originally thought, oh

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:46.579
my gosh, what is this?

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.589
And then I saw just how

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:49.759
important that work is.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.559
And it's also a lot of work that

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:53.239
most think tanks don't do.

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.189
They don't do implementation.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:57.079
They just want to debate policy

00:03:57.080 --> 00:03:58.999
ideas. So that's what made

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.559
Reason, and then the local

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.549
government center and then the

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:02.959
privatization center and center for

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.029
government reform so unique.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.949
And at that time, there was a lot

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.109
of debate really about

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:10.999
who should be providing

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:13.069
these different services.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.899
Should it be the public sector,

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.699
the private sector, sort of the

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.199
mixed thing. And there's a large

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.999
debate. That debate is is largely

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:22.369
over today.

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:24.199
My friend Steve

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.200
Sarvis

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:28.759
wrote literally 35

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.649
years ago about how government

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:33.019
should steer, not grow.

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.230
And increasingly

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:37.549
that is no longer something

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.469
that is debated, whether

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:41.149
it doesn't matter on the Republican

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.589
side, the Democrat side, what

00:04:42.590 --> 00:04:43.639
ideology you have.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:45.439
Increasingly government is playing

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.049
the role of steering

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:49.229
rather than actually.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.049
And funding as well, Right?

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:52.099
I mean, like a lot of the money.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:53.539
And one of the things that was

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:54.949
fascinating in the book, I didn't

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.839
realize you talk about

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.269
how in during the Manhattan Project,

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:00.919
like a ton of that stuff was

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.629
outsourced to private industry

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.829
because one of your previous books,

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:08.119
you know, if we can put a man on the

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.099
Moon, dot, dot, dot,

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:11.779
it's kind of there's a long

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.879
tradition going back

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.199
really centuries.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.849
In Ben Franklin's autobiography

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:18.289
talks about, you know, fulfilling a

00:05:18.290 --> 00:05:20.359
contract that he got,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.249
a governmental contract that he got

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:24.289
for delivering stuff to the

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.999
border or the frontier in the French

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.379
and Indian War and stuff.

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:28.759
But the Manhattan Project.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.229
Talk a little bit about how like

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:31.939
they farmed out different parts of

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:32.779
that.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.399
And wasn't it was the Manhattan

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.859
Project where then General

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.899
Leslie Groves

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:40.789
was the person who was in charge of

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.709
this, and he had to find

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.659
a way to build the atomic

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.639
bomb, but do it in a lab

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.589
with a lot of secrecy.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.569
And there was a little

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.429
he tens of thousands of

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.319
academic researchers and people.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.629
Both from private industry

00:05:56.630 --> 00:05:57.799
and some government who are all

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.599
working on different elements of it.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.339
And growth was one of just a few

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.109
people who knew how all the

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.149
different parts were working,

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:06.859
and that actually we saw the very

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.259
similar model in the Apollo mission

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:10.759
when we first first put a man on the

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.459
move. That was James Webb,

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.679
who listened to Kennedy's

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.569
call and and

00:06:17.570 --> 00:06:19.789
he was instrumental in organizing

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.559
this vast network.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:22.969
And when you look at the Apollo

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:26.269
mission, it was over 22,000

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:28.369
different firms that were involved

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:30.259
in that academic research

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:31.909
centers everywhere, NASA's

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:32.910
employees.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.749
And so we have this history

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:36.869
of doing big things.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:38.179
And when we do it, it's usually

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.249
through these very blended models.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:42.109
And and I think in many

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.279
respects, the U.S.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:44.809
has really led the way, you know,

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.759
probably over centuries and

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.589
and developed these sort of very

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:50.809
sophisticated network models.

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:53.209
But it's hard to find something

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:54.649
that we've done that you can say a

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.599
really big accomplishment that

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:58.519
wasn't done in this sort of a model.

00:06:59.630 --> 00:07:01.519
When you were starting

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.989
out at reason and whatnot.

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:04.759
One of the big books that was in the

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.769
air and concepts was Reinventing

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.629
Government, which

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:10.879
Al Gore and

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.019
I'm struggling to remember

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:14.059
his name, Bill Clinton.

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:16.519
You know, it's funny how quickly

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:17.509
people kind of fade from

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.339
consciousness, but they you

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:20.359
know, they were talking a lot.

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:22.219
That was part of their pitch as like

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.809
New Democrats that they were going

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:25.249
to reinvent government.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:26.629
Can you talk a little bit about that

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.089
book and how that is?

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.649
You know, that has had like a

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.329
serious influence along with, you

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:33.679
know, people like Bob Poole and

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:35.059
Steve Savage, you know, talking

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:36.739
about privatization.

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:37.879
Well, absolutely.

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:39.589
Actually, David Osborne, one of the

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.809
coauthors of Reinventing government,

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.699
actually gave us a nice blurb

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:44.959
for this book.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:46.819
But that book really did

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.799
usher in a whole wave

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:50.719
of government reform and a focus

00:07:50.720 --> 00:07:52.939
on good government, effective

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:54.619
government in a nonpartisan way

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:56.149
during the nineties. And that for

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:57.409
those of us who've been looking at

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.119
public management and that's for me,

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.319
it's three and a half decades, that

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.179
was really the heyday of a

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.339
lot of that. We saw a lot

00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.499
of very business minded mayors

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.459
who focused on pragmatic things

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.519
that make new cities run, like Steve

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.259
Goldsmith from Indianapolis, who I

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.759
wrote a book with.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:15.109
You had Ed Rendell from

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.279
Philadelphia.

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.199
There was a Democrat, Al

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:19.819
Smith, a Republican.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:20.839
And a Democrat.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:22.459
And then a lot of governors like

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.319
William Weld, who's been featured.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.729
And you've had on your show before,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.579
and John Engler and others.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.589
So it was a really big focus.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.509
And one of the reasons why got

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:34.189
so much attention, of course, was

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.229
because Bill Clinton

00:08:36.230 --> 00:08:37.579
was seen during his campaign

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.899
actually walking around with it.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:40.759
And then, of course, famously,

00:08:40.760 --> 00:08:42.589
Al Gore went on the David

00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:44.569
Letterman show and he

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.579
looked at these ashtrays that cost

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.169
so much. And then they had big

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.849
forklifts that they showed all these

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:50.959
government regulations.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:52.339
So they did a really nice job

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:53.719
messaging in kind of making

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.979
government reform sexy.

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:57.829
Yeah. What was the what was

00:08:57.830 --> 00:08:59.749
the impetus? Was it a

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:00.979
sense because this is, you know,

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.259
coming after the Reagan years

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:05.119
and, you know, Reagan was

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:07.009
a revolutionary or whatever, but

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:08.629
he was very popular.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.549
H.W. Bush,

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.229
you know, was kind of popular until

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:13.249
he lost reelection.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:16.279
But like, why were Democrats

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.139
championing a new way

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.849
of delivering government services?

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:22.549
Was it? And I guess was it because

00:09:22.550 --> 00:09:24.439
they had failed in the older

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.069
model, or were we out of money?

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:27.529
Like what were the pressures saying

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:29.269
we got to do something different?

00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:30.769
Well, I think, you know, some of it

00:09:30.770 --> 00:09:32.569
was, of course, a lot of the New

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:33.769
Democrat mantra.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:35.089
And there was thinktanks like the

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:36.949
Progressive Policy Institute,

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.809
led by Will Marshall and my friend

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:40.579
