00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:39.479
collapsed.

00:28:39.480 --> 00:28:40.499
Yeah.

00:28:40.500 --> 00:28:42.689
And outside

00:28:42.690 --> 00:28:43.690
of Philadelphia,

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:46.439
they estimated it was going to take

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:48.449
five months for

00:28:48.450 --> 00:28:50.219
it to be fixed.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:51.299
And that would have just created

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:53.579
massive congestion problems,

00:28:53.580 --> 00:28:55.409
all sorts of other issues.

00:28:55.410 --> 00:28:57.419
And Governor Josh SHAPIRO said,

00:28:57.420 --> 00:28:59.339
you know, and every single

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:00.839
regulation that gets in the way of

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.729
getting this done quickly,

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:04.979
we're going to wave at this point.

00:29:04.980 --> 00:29:06.299
And what did they do through a

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:07.979
public private partnership, mostly a

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:10.019
lot of private sector innovation,

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.849
including bringing in NASCAR

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.829
engineers to

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:14.789
help with it.

00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:16.829
They were able to get that bridge

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:18.749
fixed in 12,

00:29:18.750 --> 00:29:19.799
12 days.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.839
And one of the things that we talk

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:23.609
a lot about in the book is what are

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:25.439
the lessons we can learn from this

00:29:25.440 --> 00:29:27.149
disaster response when government is

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.369
able to move very, very quickly

00:29:29.370 --> 00:29:31.109
and you need to then rethink a lot

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.119
of the current regulations so

00:29:33.120 --> 00:29:35.399
government can be a lot more agile.

00:29:35.400 --> 00:29:37.289
So what happens, though, because,

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:38.879
you know, with things like

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:40.559
telehealth, I mean, this might be a

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:42.059
little bit different, but, you know,

00:29:42.060 --> 00:29:44.249
various states waived people

00:29:44.250 --> 00:29:45.599
having to get certified in their

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.159
state in order to practice medicine,

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.319
etc., things like that.

00:29:49.320 --> 00:29:51.539
A lot of workplace restrictions

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:52.949
and regulations, a lot of things

00:29:52.950 --> 00:29:54.809
were waived during COVID.

00:29:54.810 --> 00:29:56.459
And then they start to come back.

00:29:56.460 --> 00:29:58.799
Right. And the FDA,

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:00.659
you know, you know, the FDA

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:02.909
is not an agile organization,

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:04.289
right? And they want to go back to

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:06.119
the status quo before

00:30:06.120 --> 00:30:07.829
COVID and things like that.

00:30:07.830 --> 00:30:09.689
How do you work to,

00:30:09.690 --> 00:30:12.329
you know, lock in the liberating

00:30:12.330 --> 00:30:14.819
aspects of a de-regulated

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:16.829
kind of crisis environment?

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:18.839
And we during COVID, I've heard

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:20.669
a lot of studies on

00:30:20.670 --> 00:30:22.199
what are the lessons learned, how do

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:24.449
we overturn orthodoxies

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:26.129
and so on? Can we learn?

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:27.899
And the speed at which government

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.909
move then was kind of unprecedented

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:30.839
in many respects.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:32.039
And so it was like that.

00:30:32.040 --> 00:30:33.569
You're never going to have that over

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.249
a long period of time because we do

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:37.379
have checks and balances

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:39.209
and democratic processes and

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:40.289
so on to go forth.

00:30:40.290 --> 00:30:41.729
But what we don't want to do is go

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:43.109
back to that old way.

00:30:43.110 --> 00:30:44.249
You want to learn a lot of the

00:30:44.250 --> 00:30:45.719
lessons and hopefully have a new

00:30:45.720 --> 00:30:46.499
normal. Right.

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:48.239
And that's really the key point and

00:30:48.240 --> 00:30:50.609
new normal looking at

00:30:50.610 --> 00:30:52.559
based on lessons from those

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.169
different crises. This is.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:56.719
Do you think we've done that with,

00:30:56.720 --> 00:30:58.699
you know, it related to

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:00.529
things like vaccines or the

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:02.689
delivery of medicine post COVID?

00:31:02.690 --> 00:31:04.909
Like, have we learned lessons

00:31:04.910 --> 00:31:06.889
that are going to reset how we do

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:07.879
things?

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:10.129
We certainly are.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:12.109
My my center produces

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:14.149
50 to 60 studies a year

00:31:14.150 --> 00:31:15.589
on government management and

00:31:15.590 --> 00:31:16.459
technology.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.349
And we look a lot at public, private

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:19.879
and just around research and

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:21.769
development from the

00:31:21.770 --> 00:31:23.929
standpoint of medicine,

00:31:23.930 --> 00:31:25.399
pharmaceuticals and so on.

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:27.499
There's a lot of rethinking going on

00:31:27.500 --> 00:31:29.899
on that and how to do that better,

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:31.729
how to use more open innovation

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:34.069
models within those processes.

00:31:34.070 --> 00:31:36.409
How do we speed things up?

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:37.969
And there's actually a lot of ways

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:39.099
you can use technology.

00:31:39.100 --> 00:31:40.549
Are there like who are the

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.049
constituents for that?

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:44.359
Because it's, you know, it's

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:45.709
I'm thinking of the political

00:31:45.710 --> 00:31:48.229
dynamics and it's like during COVID,

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.359
obviously, no congressman

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:52.009
or, you know, no, no elected

00:31:52.010 --> 00:31:54.079
official wanted to be the person

00:31:54.080 --> 00:31:55.429
was like, here, put this in your

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:56.719
arm. And then like a lot of people

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:58.039
die.

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:00.139
But, you know,

00:32:00.140 --> 00:32:01.849
you know, do they have an incentive

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:03.709
to, you know,

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:05.539
once the crisis is gone to

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:06.499
be like, yeah, you know, what we

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:07.969
should be doing as we should.

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:09.469
The government should be doing less

00:32:09.470 --> 00:32:11.359
in terms of regulating or

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:14.149
or overseeing everything.

00:32:14.150 --> 00:32:16.099
I think it's less about doing

00:32:16.100 --> 00:32:17.329
less and regulate than new

00:32:17.330 --> 00:32:19.129
regulatory models and using

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:21.079
technology where I'm

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:22.549
doing a lot of work right now and

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:24.619
Gen I and I

00:32:24.620 --> 00:32:26.749
and regulation and looking at that

00:32:26.750 --> 00:32:28.639
and you can dramatically speed

00:32:28.640 --> 00:32:30.469
up all sorts of these processes

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:32.569
simply by using technology.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:34.549
I mean Markt Andreasen years ago

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:36.439
in his manifesto that software he

00:32:36.440 --> 00:32:37.789
eats every.

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:38.329
Day the.

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:39.619
Leads the world.

00:32:39.620 --> 00:32:41.509
And he also he wrote

00:32:41.510 --> 00:32:43.159
one at the beginning of COVID time

00:32:43.160 --> 00:32:45.709
to build which didn't

00:32:45.710 --> 00:32:47.839
which didn't extend to a,

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:49.729
you know, a middle

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:51.439
income housing development that was

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:52.939
adjacent to one of his properties

00:32:52.940 --> 00:32:54.829
where he tried to get it quashed.

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:55.999
But yeah.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:57.349
So it's about having regulatory

00:32:57.350 --> 00:32:59.779
processes which are more agile,

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:01.639
which are outcome based,

00:33:01.640 --> 00:33:03.019
which are looking at one of the

00:33:03.020 --> 00:33:04.549
areas that we're seeing governments

00:33:04.550 --> 00:33:06.499
all over the world do is regulatory

00:33:06.500 --> 00:33:07.309
sandboxes.

00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:09.259
We've seen that in the fintech area.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:10.249
Yeah, explain that.

00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:11.689
That's essentially where you bring

00:33:11.690 --> 00:33:13.939
in the UK has been a leader

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:15.049
in this area.

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:17.029
You bring them into a sandbox

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:18.919
essentially and you enable

00:33:18.920 --> 00:33:20.779
them to do various things

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:22.399
that previously weren't allowed

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:24.289
under close supervision.

00:33:24.290 --> 00:33:25.789
And what governments are doing is

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:27.679
they're learning from

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:28.999
how those technologies are

00:33:29.000 --> 00:33:30.229
progressing.

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:32.089
And instead of going in early with

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:34.279
precautionary regulation,

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:35.839
they're actually stepping back and

00:33:35.840 --> 00:33:36.799
they're learning in a more

00:33:36.800 --> 00:33:37.909
controlled environment.

00:33:37.910 --> 00:33:39.409
And they've even found the startups

00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:41.209
that are part of these sandboxes end

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:42.859
up getting more investment money.

00:33:42.860 --> 00:33:44.419
They do better over a long period of

00:33:44.420 --> 00:33:46.309
time. So it's a very different sort

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:47.899
of model, you know, based on more of

00:33:47.900 --> 00:33:49.789
the innovation principle of

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:50.959
regulation.

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:52.999
And I think that's a really positive

00:33:53.000 --> 00:33:54.979
things. I was on a World Economic

00:33:54.980 --> 00:33:57.079
Forum Futures Council

00:33:57.080 --> 00:33:58.789
where we had dozens of regulators

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:00.169
from all over the world who were

00:34:00.170 --> 00:34:01.639
looking at these sort of more

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:03.499
flexible models for regulating

00:34:03.500 --> 00:34:05.059
emerging technologies because

00:34:05.060 --> 00:34:07.219
they're moving too quickly

00:34:07.220 --> 00:34:09.138
for those old regulatory models to

00:34:09.139 --> 00:34:10.139
work very well.

00:34:11.179 --> 00:34:13.189
What what do we do about,

00:34:13.190 --> 00:34:14.689
you know, one of the things where

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:16.399
there seems to be a new normal, I

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:18.468
kind of referenced that earlier.

00:34:18.469 --> 00:34:20.299
You know, we went from I think

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:22.488
it was in 2019, the federal

00:34:22.489 --> 00:34:24.769
budget was like, I think $4.8

00:34:24.770 --> 00:34:26.629
trillion, and

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:28.459
then it went up over 6 trillion

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:30.169
very quickly because of COVID,

00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:31.459
because of a lot of federal

00:34:31.460 --> 00:34:32.448
spending.

00:34:32.449 --> 00:34:34.339
And then it went up to 6.8

00:34:34.340 --> 00:34:35.959
trillion in 2021.

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:37.279
And, you know, now it's down a

00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:38.178
little bit from that.

00:34:38.179 --> 00:34:40.099
But, you know, this is the kind of

00:34:40.100 --> 00:34:42.138
Robert Higgs crisis and Leviathan

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:43.638
model that during a crisis,

00:34:43.639 --> 00:34:45.649
government expands, spending

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:47.178
expands, and then it might come down

00:34:47.179 --> 00:34:48.619
a little bit, but it doesn't come

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:51.259
back to where it was, how,

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:53.149
you know, how do we steer

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:54.619
clear of that kind of problem?

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:58.129
Well, my focus for the

00:34:58.130 --> 00:34:59.629
last couple of decades has really

00:34:59.630 --> 00:35:01.609
been on making

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:02.839
government work better.

00:35:02.840 --> 00:35:04.669
Right. And as opposed

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:06.709
to the the overall size, I

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:08.659
think those are those are a lot of

00:35:08.660 --> 00:35:09.829
policy decisions.

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:11.539
They're a lot of based on first

00:35:11.540 --> 00:35:12.769
principles.

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:14.449
We don't seem to be having that

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:16.359
debate in America

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:17.869
or many other countries around the

00:35:17.870 --> 00:35:19.459
world right now.

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.409
And I think, you know,

00:35:21.410 --> 00:35:23.509
depending on where interest rates go

00:35:23.510 --> 00:35:25.189
and the debt situation, that may

00:35:25.190 --> 00:35:27.079
bring a lot of that back again.

00:35:27.080 --> 00:35:28.939
But as you've seen with the

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.249
model that we have here,

00:35:31.250 --> 00:35:33.169
if you're really going to talk about

00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:35.389
the size of government and so on,

00:35:35.390 --> 00:35:36.949
it needs to be based on where the

00:35:36.950 --> 00:35:39.019
government spending actually is

00:35:39.020 --> 00:35:41.179
today as opposed to where most

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:42.889
of the debates are on, which is a

00:35:42.890 --> 00:35:44.989
very, very tiny fraction

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:46.399
of the U.S. budget.

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:48.229
What do you you know, how

00:35:48.230 --> 00:35:50.149
do you I don't know

00:35:50.150 --> 00:35:52.099
if you consider yourself like,

00:35:52.100 --> 00:35:53.039
you know, a.

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:55.019
Libertarian or, you know, a

00:35:55.020 --> 00:35:57.179
small government conservative

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.129
or or, you know,

00:35:59.130 --> 00:36:00.329
a small government liberal or

00:36:00.330 --> 00:36:01.529
progressive or whatever.

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:03.569
But what

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.579
you know, if government gets better,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:07.469
I guess this is my question is

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:09.179
like, if government gets better,

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:11.939
will people demand more of it?

00:36:11.940 --> 00:36:13.859
Because I know a lot of libertarians

00:36:13.860 --> 00:36:15.719
so that when you say and I've heard

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:17.339
this, you know, and people like Bob

00:36:17.340 --> 00:36:19.049
Paul and people at Reason Foundation

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.119
who help implement better

00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:22.859
government policies, people you

00:36:22.860 --> 00:36:24.329
know, I know a lot of libertarians

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:26.189
who will say, you know, why would

00:36:26.190 --> 00:36:27.119
you want to do that? I want

00:36:27.120 --> 00:36:28.679
government to suck as hard as

00:36:28.680 --> 00:36:30.539
possible because then people

00:36:30.540 --> 00:36:31.540
will get rid of it.

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:34.919
Is this is that borne out by data

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.679
or, you know, explain to,

00:36:37.680 --> 00:36:39.209
you know, libertarian like through a

00:36:39.210 --> 00:36:41.369
narco capitalist why it's actually

00:36:41.370 --> 00:36:43.199
worth their time to

00:36:43.200 --> 00:36:44.429
have a government that, you know,

00:36:44.430 --> 00:36:46.049
runs its schools well?

00:36:46.050 --> 00:36:47.789
Well, this is a great discussion.

00:36:47.790 --> 00:36:49.649
And we we had this discussion over a

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:50.939
decade ago.

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:52.919
Yeah. And and I actually

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:54.989
even did a piece Why

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:56.969
libertarians should stop hating

00:36:56.970 --> 00:36:58.529
government kind of because actually

00:36:58.530 --> 00:37:00.569
the data is pretty clear

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:02.639
that those governments, when

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:04.679
you have worse government, more

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:06.239
bureaucratic government and so on,

00:37:06.240 --> 00:37:07.349
government can't get things done.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:09.179
It ends up being bigger, growing

00:37:09.180 --> 00:37:10.199
bigger over time.

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:12.299
Which is very counterintuitive, but.

00:37:12.300 --> 00:37:14.279
It's because people keep putting

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:15.899
more and more money into things and

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:17.039
they're not working. And then you

00:37:17.040 --> 00:37:18.869
have all these processes and

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:20.519
bureaucratic and sclerotic process.

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:22.559
A lot of rent seeking involved,

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:24.419
a lot of bureaucracy and

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:26.399
so on. So in fact, and just think

00:37:26.400 --> 00:37:28.139
about things in our everyday lives

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:29.909
that it does make a big difference.

00:37:29.910 --> 00:37:31.919
So I ask a lot of people, what's

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:33.779
the biggest innovation in government

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:35.249
in the last decade or so that has

00:37:35.250 --> 00:37:36.629
made a difference in your lives?

00:37:36.630 --> 00:37:38.399
And for business travelers, it's

00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:40.529
things like TSA pre

00:37:40.530 --> 00:37:42.389
and global entry

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.279
and even clear which is more of

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.439
a private sector. I, I just traveled

00:37:46.440 --> 00:37:47.549
to Europe.

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:48.689
I was in London.

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:51.089
I was in Paris and then back here

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.009
I breezed through the airport

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:54.869
in ways that was similar to pre

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:57.089
911 and that was were based

00:37:57.090 --> 00:37:59.369
on having these systems

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:00.749
that broke trade offs.

00:38:00.750 --> 00:38:02.039
Innovation is about breaking trade

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.199
offs. So TSA PreCheck global

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:05.399
entry, they broke that trade up

00:38:05.400 --> 00:38:07.259
between having more security

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:08.729
and convenience and they said we can

00:38:08.730 --> 00:38:10.049
actually have both.

00:38:10.050 --> 00:38:12.119
So there are a lot of different ways

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:13.949
that by reducing friction,

00:38:13.950 --> 00:38:15.929
by making government better, more

00:38:15.930 --> 00:38:18.059
efficient, it can actually improve

00:38:18.060 --> 00:38:19.919
our lives. It can actually lead to

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:21.659
a much better business climate.

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:23.609
I mean, think about the countries

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:24.839
where government doesn't work at

00:38:24.840 --> 00:38:27.029
all. Very rarely is that business

00:38:27.030 --> 00:38:29.489
climate very good either,

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:31.499
because there's so much friction in

00:38:31.500 --> 00:38:33.329
the system or in

00:38:33.330 --> 00:38:34.559
some of those countries where then

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:36.449
you have a lot of both corruption

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:38.279
in the system. So, you know,

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:40.019
I've always believed that better

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:42.209
government can lead to more freedom

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:44.369
for individuals, a better economic

00:38:44.370 --> 00:38:45.779
climate for businesses.

00:38:45.780 --> 00:38:47.279
And I think the data shows that out

00:38:47.280 --> 00:38:47.979
pretty clearly.

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:49.559
Yeah, it's a couple of years ago,

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:50.639
more than a few.

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:53.039
I wrote a story about how,

00:38:53.040 --> 00:38:54.629
you know, it's widely observed by

00:38:54.630 --> 00:38:56.369
economists and sociologists and

00:38:56.370 --> 00:38:58.439
whatnot around the globe that as

00:38:58.440 --> 00:39:00.449
societies go from high trust to low

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:02.459
trust, you know, corruption

00:39:02.460 --> 00:39:03.869
